    <blockquote>
    <h2>Tink Java v1.24.0</h2>
    <p>Tink is a multi-language, cross-platform library that provides simple
    and
    misuse-proof APIs for common cryptographic tasks.</p>
    <p><strong>This is Tink Java 1.24.0</strong></p>
    <p>The complete list of changes since 1.23.0 can be found <a
    href="https://github.com/tink-crypto/tink-java/compare/v1.23.0...v1.24.0">here</a>.</p>
    <h2>New Features</h2>
    <ul>
    <li>
    <p>APIs which depend on what algorithms are registered now have an
    overload taking a configuration argument. Using these overloads allows
    to ensure that behavior is independent of what else is linked in the
    binary. For example, instead of
    <code>keysetHandle.getPrimitive(Aead.class)</code>, users can now write
    <code>keysetHandle.getPrimitive(TinkConfig2026.get(),
    Aead.class)</code>. Similary for key generation and serialization of
    keysets.</p>
    <p>The following configs are available: <code>AeadConfig2026</code>
    (which does not contain the KMS algorithms; use
    <code>KmsAeadConfig2026</code> for those),
    <code>DeterministicAeadConfig2026</code>, <code>HybridConfig2026</code>,
    <code>JwtConfig2026</code>, <code>KeyDerivationConfig2026</code>,
    <code>MacConfig2026</code>, <code>PrfConfig2026</code>,
    <code>SignatureConfig2026</code>, <code>StreamingAeadConfig2026</code>.
    In addition, <code>TinkConfig2026</code> contains all of the above
    algorithms (except the KMS algorithms). Finally,
    <code>RegistryConfiguration.java</code> is available to make previous
    behavior (examining the registered algorithms) explicit.</p>
    <p>Finally, the <code>Configuration</code> interface needed to implement
    new behavior is completely public, and users can implement it
    themselves.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
    <p>Added support for <code>JWT ML-DSA</code> signatures.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
    <p>Added support for <code>MLDSA44-Ed25519-SHA512</code> and
    <code>MLDSA65-Ed25519-SHA512</code> Composite ML-DSA algorithms.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
    <p>X-Wing HPKE is now also available on Android. In particular, it is
    available natively on Android 17 (or above), or otherwise on Android 12
    (or above) with SDK Extensions version 22 or later.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
    <p>Added predefined HPKE parameters.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
    <p>Added the <code>Prehash</code> primitive interface for computing
    prehashes of messages, to support the prehash-and-sign paradigm (such as
    ML-DSA External Mu).</p>
    </li>
    <li>
    <p>Updated 'org.conscrypt:conscrypt-openjdk-uber' to version 2.7.0.</p>
    </li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <ul>
    <li>Changed the named parameter name for SLH-DSA-SHA2-128S with Tink
    output prefix type from <code>SLH_DSA_SHA2_128S_TINK</code> to
    <code>SLH_DSA_SHA2_128S</code> for consistency with the other Tink
    languages.</li>
    <li>In <code>SignaturePemKeysetReader</code>, treat Base64 decoding
    failures like other PEM key parsing errors.</li>
    <li>In <code>SignaturePemKeysetReader</code>, continue parsing multi-key
    PEM bundles after encountering an unparsable key.</li>
    <li>In <code>SignaturePemKeysetReader</code>, preserve the parsed RSA
    public exponent when importing RSA public keys instead of hardcoding
    <code>65537</code> (<code>F4</code>).</li>
    <li>Fixed unchecked runtime exceptions in
    <code>JwkSetConverter.toPublicKeysetHandle</code> and
    <code>SignatureJwkSetConverter.toPublicKeysetHandle</code> when
    importing malformed JWK sets, ensuring they consistently throw a
    <code>GeneralSecurityException</code>.</li>
    <li>Validate coordinate lengths against curve field size in
    <code>JwkSetConverter</code> and
    <code>SignatureJwkSetConverter</code>.</li>
    <li>Fixed unchecked runtime exceptions in
    <code>JsonKeysetReader.read</code> and
    <code>JsonKeysetReader.readEncrypted</code> when reading malformed
    keysets, ensuring they consistently throw an
    <code>IOException</code>.</li>
    <li><code>AesGcmSiv</code> now uses Conscrypt if it's available on the
    class path, even if it is not installed. Previously, a Conscrypt
    provider needed to be installed for it to work.</li>
    <li>Enforce canonical Base64url encoding in
    <code>JwtFormat.strictUrlSafeDecode</code>, preventing signature/MAC
    malleability from unused trailing bits.</li>
    <li>Fixed <code>StreamingAeadSeekableDecryptingChannel.read</code> to
    always authenticate the final ciphertext segment when reaching the end
    of the plaintext, ensuring valid empty plaintexts return <code>-1</code>
    (EOF) and rejecting truncated or unauthenticated empty trailing segments
    with an <code>IOException</code>.</li>
    </ul>
